  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e3a2134d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Observation\n",
    "\n",
    "- Logistic Regression with `class_weight='balanced'` raised recall on the churn class from **57% to 80%**, but accuracy dropped from **80.31% to 72.85%** and precision on the churn class fell from **65% to 49%**. ROC-AUC stayed essentially unchanged (0.836), confirming that class weighting shifts the decision boundary rather than the model's underlying ranking ability.\n",
    "- Random Forest with `class_weight='balanced'` barely changed: recall actually moved slightly from **52% to 49%**, while accuracy and ROC-AUC stayed roughly the same. Tree ensembles are less sensitive to class weighting than linear models in this case.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Business Insight\n",
    "\n",
    "Class weighting is a useful lever specifically for Logistic Regression here: it substantially increases the number of true churners caught, at a real cost in false alarms and overall accuracy. Whether this trade-off is worth it depends on the relative cost of losing a customer versus the cost of an unnecessary retention offer (see Section 14 for a more direct threshold-based version of this trade-off, which gives finer control than a single weighting scheme)."
   ]
  },
